      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 11,
        "titulo": "The adjective agrees in gender and number",
        "explicacao": "Listen first: «petit» and «petite». The difference is the last letter. STEP 1: in French the adjective agrees with the subject. If the person is a woman, the adjective goes into the feminine. In general, you add «e». «Content» becomes «contente». «Petit» becomes «petite». STEP 2: the «e» changes the sound. When it appears, the final consonant is pronounced. «Petit» sounds like «puh-TEE». «Petite» sounds like «puh-TEET». This is the sound that shows the feminine in speech. STEP 3: adjectives that already end in «e» do not change: «triste», «jeune», «rouge». STEP 4: some endings change more. «Brésilien» becomes «brésilienne». «Heureux» becomes «heureuse». STEP 5: in the plural, you add «s», which is not pronounced. «Petit» becomes «petits», but both sound like «puh-TEE». If it already ends in «s», it stays the same: «français», «français». STEP 6: with «on», the adjective can go into the plural: «On est contents».",
        "comparacao": "English adjectives never change (\"tired\" is always \"tired\"), so this is new: in French the adjective changes with the noun, «fatigué» and «fatiguée». And the ending changes the sound in a way English does not: the feminine «e» makes the final consonant audible, and the plural «s» stays silent.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Il est petit.",
            "pt": "He is small."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Elle est petite.",
            "pt": "She is small."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Il est français.",
            "pt": "He is French."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Elle est française.",
            "pt": "She is French."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Elle est heureuse.",
            "pt": "She is happy."
          },
          {
            "ru": "Elles sont contentes.",
            "pt": "They (f.) are pleased."
          }
        ],
